# Working with Objects

```elixir
System.put_env("AL_MNESIA_DISTRIBUTED", "false")

System.put_env(
  "AL_MNESIA_DIR",
  Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "al_livebook_#{:erlang.unique_integer([:positive])}")
)

Mix.install(
  [{:al, github: "anoma/AL-Ex"}],
  config: [al: [transaction_programs: [AL.TransactionProgram.Bootstrap], natives: []]]
)
```

## Setup

This notebook is self-contained. The cell above installs AL directly, no separate node, no cookie, nothing to attach to. The first run compiles AL and its dependencies from scratch, which takes a minute or two. Reopening this notebook later is fast, since `Mix.install` caches by the exact dependency and config combination. State here is throwaway. It lives only for this notebook's session and disappears when the session ends.

If you already have a real AL node running and want the persistent, shared store instead, see the [intro](./intro.livemd) for how to attach to it instead.

`Mix.install` has to finish running before AL's macros (`run`, `defclass`, and the rest) can be brought into scope, so that happens in its own cell here.

```elixir
use AL

AL.Branch.head()
```

## Creating durable classes and instances

AL has an object system in the style of CLOS and Smalltalk, and aims to make writing live, open polymorphisms easy. The details of this will become more apparent later, but the basic ideas you need to know right now are that you can create classes with `defclass`, and instances of those classes with `new`. Let's try the well-known example of a light switch, which has on/off state.

We use the `redef` flag so that the class & instance can be redefined by re-evaluating the block. Inside `:flick` below you'll see `get_slot`/`set_slot`, which read and write an object's own state. They're ordinary methods on `:object`, not special syntax, so `get_slot(obj, :key, value)` is itself a message send, the same as `flick(self)` is.

```elixir
run do
  defclass :switch,
    super: :object,
    redef: true,
    ivars: [state: [domain: ["on", "off"], default: "off"]] do

    defmethod(:flick, [self]) do
      get_slot(self, :state, "on")
      set_slot(self, :state, "off")
    end
    
    defmethod(:flick, [self]) do
      get_slot(self, :state, "off")
      set_slot(self, :state, "on")
    end
  end

  new(:switch, %{name: :switch_instance_1, state: "on", redef: true}, s)
end
```

In AL, durable objects are represented by an ID, which currently takes the form of an atom (soon, we will change these to binaries, as it can eventually fill up the atom table). You can inspect basic information about a durable object using examine/2.

```elixir
run do
  examine(:switch_instance_1, info)
end
```

```elixir
run do
  examine(:switch, info)
end
```

As you can see, `:switch` is a class which inherits from `:object`. It provides the method `:flick`, which we defined. It was used to create `:switch_instance_1`. `:switch`es have a 'state' which is either on or off.

`examine` isn't the only way to see this. `class/2` and `super/2` are themselves ordinary queryable relations, not just something `defclass` declares once and hides away.

```elixir
run do
  class(:switch_instance_1, c)
  super(:switch, s)
end
```

Let's see what happens if we 'flick' the 'switch'.

```elixir
run do
  flick(:switch_instance_1)
  get_slot(:switch_instance_1, :state, state)
end
```

It should work! And if you evaluate again, you'll see the state change too. `flick(:switch_instance_1)` is shorthand: it's exactly the same as writing the send out explicitly.

```elixir
run do
  send(:switch_instance_1, :flick, [])
  get_slot(:switch_instance_1, :state, state)
end
```

AL is also capable of enforcing slot invariants.

```elixir
run do
  set_slot(:switch_instance_1, :state, :sideways)
end
```

## Inheritance

As you may have already figured out, AL supports inheritance. This is not the scope to explain how inheritance itself works, but instead we will demonstrate a couple features that may be of benefit. For example, you can use `call_next_method` for method continuation and rely on inherited slots.

```elixir
run do 
  defclass :counted_switch,
    redef: true,
    super: :switch,
    ivars: [count: [type: :number, default: 0]] do
    
    defmethod(:flick, [self]) do
      get_slot(self, :count, c)
      is(c1, c + 1)
      set_slot(self, :count, c1)
      call_next_method(self)
    end
   end

  new(:counted_switch, %{name: :counted_switch_instance_1, redef: true}, c)
end
```

```elixir
run do 
  flick(:counted_switch_instance_1)
  examine(:counted_switch_instance_1, info)
end

```

In fact, AL even supports multiple inheritance. Let's take a look at the classic diamond inheritance problem. To demonstrate this, we'll need another kind of switch. Let's take one that flips its colour every time that it is turned on. For now we have a couple of limitations: Firstly, we must label the var, since constraints aren't durable in the database. Secondly, we don't have an ordering on the domain, meaning we can't express an exact cycle.

```elixir
run do
  defclass :colour_cycle_switch,
    redef: true,
    super: :switch,
    ivars: [colour: [domain: ["red", "green", "blue"]]] do

    defmethod(:flick, [self]) do
      get_slot(self, :state, "off")
      get_slot(self, :colour, c)
      in_domain(c1, ["red", "green", "blue"])
      dif(c, c1)
      label(c1)
      set_slot(self, :colour, c1)
      call_next_method(self)
    end
    
    defmethod(:flick, [self]) do
      get_slot(self, :state, "on")
      call_next_method(self)
    end
  end

  new(:colour_cycle_switch, %{name: :cc_switch_instance_1, colour: "red", redef: true}, c)
end
```

```elixir
run do
  flick(:cc_switch_instance_1)
  examine(:cc_switch_instance_1, info)
end
```

Regardless this is sufficient for demonstrating how AL manages diamond inheritance. Here's a little tip: you can search a method by its object-associated name using the following snippet. For example:

```elixir
run do
  findall([obj, id], [vm_method(obj, :flick, id)], objids)
end
```

```elixir
run do
  defclass :diamond_switch,
    redef: true,
    super: [:counted_switch, :colour_cycle_switch] do
  end

  new(:diamond_switch, %{name: :diamond_switch_instance_1, colour: "red", state: "off", redef: true}, c)
end
```

The order in which the two supers are topologically sorted actually doesn't matter for flick, since the operations we defined are commutative.

```elixir
run do
  flick(:diamond_switch_instance_1)
  examine(:diamond_switch_instance_1, info)
end
```

## Categories: importing methods without inheritance

`categories:` is a different way to share methods, and it isn't inheritance at all. A category is just an ordinary object with some methods on it. `import` copies those methods directly onto a class by their shared method id, with no super edge created — the class's own super chain is completely unaffected.

```elixir
run do
  new(:category, %{name: :glowable, super: :object, ivars: []}, cat)

  defmethod(:glowable, :glow, [self, result]) do
    unify(result, :shiny)
  end

  defclass :lamp, super: :object, categories: [:glowable] do
  end

  new(:lamp, %{name: :lamp_1}, w)
  glow(:lamp_1, result)
end
```

`:lamp` answers `:glow`, but its super is still just `:object`.

```elixir
run do
  super(:lamp, s)
end
```

## Printing objects

Every object can describe itself for printing, through a relation called `:print_object`. It doesn't print anything by itself. It just relates `self` to some representation of it, and something else decides what to do with that (like `listing` below). The default version, defined on `:object`, just reports the object's own class.

```elixir
run do
  print_object(:switch_instance_1, description)
end
```

Any class can override `:print_object` to describe its own instances better, the same way `printOn:` works in Smalltalk. We can add this straight to `:switch` without touching its existing `:flick` clauses, since `defmethod` also works as a standalone call, not only nested inside `defclass`.

```elixir
run do
  defmethod(:switch, :print_object, [self, description]) do
    get_slot(self, :state, s)
    unify(description, {:switch, s})
  end

  print_object(:switch_instance_1, description)
end
```

`vm_format` (see [syntax basics](./syntax_basics.livemd)) has a directive for exactly this two-step dance. `~o` resolves `print_object` on its argument automatically, then formats the result, so the cell above is the same as writing this in one step:

```elixir
run do
  vm_format("~o~%", [:switch_instance_1])
end
```

Methods are printable objects too. `:behaviour`, the class every class, category and method eventually falls under, overrides `:print_object` to return a method's own source text instead of a class name. `listing/2` uses that to print every clause of a method: give it a class and a method name, and it finds the method and runs `print_object` on each of its clauses.

```elixir
run do
  listing(:switch, :flick)
end
```

## Ephemeral Objects (Values)

So far we have only discussed 'durable' objects, without defining what this means. Each durable object in AL has a backing existence in the underlying Mnesia relation tables (which as you will see later, is just a RAM-based view over the command log).

Ephemeral/Value objects, on the other hand, only exist for the lifespan of a transaction, which means they do not clog up the database and are perfect for calculation. This means new objects can be generated 'on the fly' without dirtying the database.

A nice thing about ephemerals is that `new` gets to decide what the representation of an ephemeral actually is. In fact, numbers and lists are both ephemerals.

```elixir
run do
  super(:number, :value)
end
```

We'll discuss the most interesting facets of ephemerals in [logic and constraints](./logic_and_constraints.livemd).

## Advanced: raw method dispatch

Everything so far has gone through ordinary dispatch: a send looks up which method answers a selector on a class, following its supers if it has to. `vm_oapply` skips inheritance chaining. You give it a method's own internal id directly, found with `vm_method`, and it runs that exact method body, bypassing class/selector lookup entirely.

```elixir
run do
  vm_method(:number, :factorial, id)
  vm_oapply(id, [5, result])
end
```

This is how AL implements things like `call_next_method` under the hood. It is not advised to use this in ordinary code, although it may provide performance boosts since it does not require checking the inheritance chain.

## Some philosophy on 'Relational Objects'

As you may have noticed, relations about objects don't require the object to 'exist' in some sense. In fact, to say that an object exists in AL is to say that there are relations which concern that object: The ID is meaningless in itself, and is just a handle or pointer to establish sameness- the essence of the object lies in its relations.